                {
                    "author": {
                        "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                        "login": "echasnovski",
                        "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                        "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
                    },
                    "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
                    "body": "> How to do that in the most reliable and easy way?\r\n\r\nI am afraid \"reliable\" and \"easy\" here are a bit at odds with each other.\r\n\r\nThe most reliable way would be to use [tree-sitter based surrounding](https://nvim-mini.org/mini.nvim/doc/mini-surround.html#minisurround.gen_spec.input.treesitter). For *this particular case*, it seems to require custom tree-sitter queries.\r\n\r\nHere is what I've got mostly working:\r\n\r\n- Write custom tree-sitter query with two captures: one for the outside (text + left surrounding + right surrounding) and one for the inside (only text without surroundings). For this particular case, put the following code into '~/.config/nvim/after/queries/go/textobjects.scm':\r\n\r\n    ```scm\r\n    ;; extends\r\n    (if_statement consequence: (block (statement_list) @if.inner )) @if.outer\r\n    ```\r\n\r\n    This query was created by trial and error using output of `:InspectTree` and `:EditQuery`.\r\n\r\n- Create 'mini.surround' config that is local to Go files. That is, in the file '~/.config/nvim/after/ftplugin/go.lua' add the following:\r\n\r\n    ```lua\r\n    if _G.MiniSurround ~= nil then\r\n      vim.b.minisurround_config = {\r\n        custom_surroundings = {\r\n          o = {\r\n            input = MiniSurround.gen_spec.input.treesitter({ outer = '@if.outer', inner = '@if.inner' }),\r\n          },\r\n        },\r\n      }\r\n    end\r\n    ```\r\n\r\nNow after restart, open the Go file and type `sho`. It should highlight the left and right surroundings. Typing `sdo` should delete it. There seem to be issues with whitespace, but that is the best I could do with the tree-sitter queries.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nAlternatively, you can also take a look at \"unscope\" example of #1951.",
                    "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">How to do that in the most reliable and easy way?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I am afraid \"reliable\" and \"easy\" here are a bit at odds with each other.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The most reliable way would be to use <a href=\"https://nvim-mini.org/mini.nvim/doc/mini-surround.html#minisurround.gen_spec.input.treesitter\" rel=\"nofollow\">tree-sitter based surrounding</a>. For <em>this particular case</em>, it seems to require custom tree-sitter queries.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Here is what I've got mostly working:</p>\n<ul dir=\"auto\">\n<li>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Write custom tree-sitter query with two captures: one for the outside (text + left surrounding + right surrounding) and one for the inside (only text without surroundings). For this particular case, put the following code into '~/.config/nvim/after/queries/go/textobjects.scm':</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-scheme notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\";; extends\n(if_statement consequence: (block (statement_list) @if.inner )) @if.outer\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-c\"><span class=\"pl-c\">;</span>; extends</span>\n(if_statement consequence: (block (statement_list) @if.inner )) @if.outer</pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">This query was created by trial and error using output of <code class=\"notranslate\">:InspectTree</code> and <code class=\"notranslate\">:EditQuery</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Create 'mini.surround' config that is local to Go files. That is, in the file '~/.config/nvim/after/ftplugin/go.lua' add the following:</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"if _G.MiniSurround ~= nil then\n  vim.b.minisurround_config = {\n    custom_surroundings = {\n      o = {\n        input = MiniSurround.gen_spec.input.treesitter({ outer = '@if.outer', inner = '@if.inner' }),\n      },\n    },\n  }\nend\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-k\">if</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">_G</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">MiniSurround</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">~=</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">nil</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">then</span>\n  <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">b</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">minisurround_config</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> {\n    <span class=\"pl-smi\">custom_surroundings</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> {\n      <span class=\"pl-smi\">o</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> {\n        <span class=\"pl-smi\">input</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">MiniSurround</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">gen_spec</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">input</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">treesitter</span>({ <span class=\"pl-smi\">outer</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>@if.outer<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">inner</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>@if.inner<span class=\"pl-pds\">' </span></span>}),\n      },\n    },\n  }\n<span class=\"pl-k\">end</span></pre></div>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Now after restart, open the Go file and type <code class=\"notranslate\">sho</code>. It should highlight the left and right surroundings. Typing <code class=\"notranslate\">sdo</code> should delete it. There seem to be issues with whitespace, but that is the best I could do with the tree-sitter queries.</p>\n<hr>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Alternatively, you can also take a look at \"unscope\" example of <a class=\"issue-link js-issue-link\" data-error-text=\"Failed to load title\" data-id=\"8748309\" data-permission-text=\"Title is private\" data-url=\"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/discussions/1951\" data-hovercard-type=\"discussion\" data-hovercard-url=\"/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/discussions/1951/hovercard\" href=\"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/discussions/1951\">#1951</a>.</p>",
                    "bodyText": "How to do that in the most reliable and easy way?\n\nI am afraid \"reliable\" and \"easy\" here are a bit at odds with each other.\nThe most reliable way would be to use tree-sitter based surrounding. For this particular case, it seems to require custom tree-sitter queries.\nHere is what I've got mostly working:\n\n\nWrite custom tree-sitter query with two captures: one for the outside (text + left surrounding + right surrounding) and one for the inside (only text without surroundings). For this particular case, put the following code into '~/.config/nvim/after/queries/go/textobjects.scm':\n;; extends\n(if_statement consequence: (block (statement_list) @if.inner )) @if.outer\nThis query was created by trial and error using output of :InspectTree and :EditQuery.\n\n\nCreate 'mini.surround' config that is local to Go files. That is, in the file '~/.config/nvim/after/ftplugin/go.lua' add the following:\nif _G.MiniSurround ~= nil then\n  vim.b.minisurround_config = {\n    custom_surroundings = {\n      o = {\n        input = MiniSurround.gen_spec.input.treesitter({ outer = '@if.outer', inner = '@if.inner' }),\n      },\n    },\n  }\nend\n\n\nNow after restart, open the Go file and type sho. It should highlight the left and right surroundings. Typing sdo should delete it. There seem to be issues with whitespace, but that is the best I could do with the tree-sitter queries.\n\nAlternatively, you can also take a look at \"unscope\" example of #1951.",
